// std/block.momo - the block DOS gave us, and whether a region fits in it
// (DESIGN §13, §16, §35, §40, §47).
//
// Three routines over the word `arena` already reads. §13 says the robust
// `_hsize` reads the end-of-allocation segment from `PSP:0x0002`, and `arena`
// proved that word reaches two thirds of a megabyte past our own segment on the
// target, with no DOS call at all. This is that, with names on it, so no program
// has to re-derive it.
//
// **It is not an allocator, and there is no `free`.** §40 settles that: "`view`
// (§17) is often the better answer... A program that knows its regions at
// compile time should not be allocating." A mode 13h back buffer is exactly that
// program - one region, 64,000 bytes, known when it is written:
//
//   include "lib/std/block.momo"
//
//   u16 bufSeg
//   far u8[64000] backBuffer = bufSeg
//
//   bufSeg = blockBase()
//   if ( !blockFits( bufSeg, 64000 ) ) { ... no room, say so and stop ... }
//
// The program declares the region and the library only says where, because a
// `far` declaration is top-level with a constant size and no routine can return
// one. A segment is the only runtime currency §16 offers, so segments are what
// these hand back, and paragraphs are the granularity because the machine's are.
//
// **Failure is 0.** DOS never loads a `.COM` at segment 0 - `dstest` already
// rests on that - so 0 cannot be a real answer, and needs no second return value
// in a language with no exceptions.

// Our own PSP as words, so offset 2 is index 1.
//
// `_ds` is the segment directly, which is what makes this file twenty lines and
// no startup sequence. §16 allows a segment register as a source, and §35 makes
// `_ds` the only one - the read is `mov dx, ds`, the same two instructions a
// constant segment costs. Before §47 that was a variable holding `_ds`, which a
// library can only fill from a top-level statement, and no other file in
// `shared/lib/std/` has one. DECISIONS §47 has why that was worth ten lines of
// compiler.
//
// `local`, because a program that wants the block wants these routines rather
// than a window onto its own PSP - the same call `time.momo` makes about the
// BIOS data area. `const`, because DOS wrote it and nothing here should be able
// to write it back.
local const far u16[2] blockPsp = _ds

// Segment one past the end of the block DOS gave us. `_hsize` (§13) assumes the
// full 64 KB and is the conservative floor; this is what DOS actually said.
u16 blockEnd() => blockPsp[1]

// First segment past our own addressable 64 KB. Our own segment holds the image,
// the stack and the heap, and every byte of it is reachable without a segment
// register - so this is where memory that needs `far` begins.
//
// 0 when there is none, which is a block of one segment or less.
//
// The test is on the size of our own block rather than on `_ds + 0x1000`, and
// that is not a stylistic choice: a program loaded above 0xF000 wraps that sum,
// and one loaded more than a paragraph above it wraps to a small non-zero
// segment, which reads as an answer. Subtracting cannot wrap, because DOS cannot
// end our block below where it started it - the invariant `arena` prints. And
// the sum below cannot wrap either, because it has just been shown to be under
// `blockEnd()`.
u16 blockBase() {
  if ( blockEnd() - _ds <= 0x1000 ) return 0

  return _ds + 0x1000
}

// Bytes rounded up to paragraphs.
//
// **Not `( bytes + 15 ) >> 4`.** That overflows a `u16` above 65,520, which is
// inside the range a caller can pass: 65,521 bytes becomes 0 paragraphs, and a
// region too large to fit anywhere reports that it fits everywhere. The failure
// is silent and it is the wrong way round, which is the worst pair available.
//
// `local`, and §47's own reasoning is why: it rejected paragraphs in the
// interface because "every caller writes the rounding by hand, which is where
// the trap lives". Exporting this would be the same mistake one step further in.
local const u16 blockParas( u16 bytes ) = ( bytes >> 4 ) + u16( ( bytes & 15 ) != 0 )

// Does a region of `bytes` fit at segment `seg`, inside the block DOS gave us?
//
// False below `blockBase()`, and that is what makes the two compose: a failed
// `blockBase()` returns 0, and 0 must not be a place where anything fits. It
// also keeps the floor stated once - a caller cannot reach under our own segment
// by passing a low number, which §40 warns is how a fuzzy boundary between two
// allocators corrupts a heap.
//
// The space remaining is compared against rather than the region's end computed,
// so a high `seg` cannot wrap and come back true.
bool blockFits( u16 seg, u16 bytes ) {
  u16 base
  u16 end

  base = blockBase()
  end = blockEnd()

  if ( base == 0 ) return false
  if ( seg < base ) return false
  if ( seg >= end ) return false

  return blockParas( bytes ) <= end - seg
}
